INT. FISH MARKET OFFICE - EARLY MORNING

A glass-walled office above the ice floor. Fluorescent hum. A wall calendar of tuna.

MIRIAM KOSKI, 50s, the market's bookkeeper, sits at a steel desk. Her ledger is open. Her cardigan smells faintly of brine.

Across from her sits DETECTIVE ANSEL WOO, 40s, notebook out. He smiles pleasantly. Behind him, leaning on the filing cabinet, is his partner DETECTIVE RUTH DELACROIX, 30s, silent, chewing gum.

Near the door stands the owner, ENZO FERRARA, 60s, in rubber boots, wiping his hands on a rag. He has been wiping them for some time.

WOO
Quiet morning.

MIRIAM
They start at four.

WOO
And the books balance.

MIRIAM
They always balance.

Ruth pushes off the cabinet and drifts to the window. Enzo steps aside to let her pass, and as she goes by she picks a pen off the desk, rolls it in her fingers, sets it back. Miriam watches the pen. Woo flips a page of the notebook. Enzo tosses the rag onto a chair, crosses the room to the door, and locks it, quietly, with a click that makes Miriam's shoulders lift. Ruth turns from the window.

RUTH
Miriam. Where's the second ledger?

MIRIAM
There isn't a second.

RUTH
She says there isn't.

WOO
(to Enzo)
He says there isn't, too.

ENZO
(tight smile)
There isn't.

Miriam lowers her eyes to the ledger. Her finger finds a column, and a drop of sweat falls onto the page.
